{"kind":"request","text":"BO2: recipes over hashes. Second slice of the boundary-objects design\nnote (notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md, landed 8659af46),\nchild of request 0e06e751. After BO1, and after declared views reach\nthe generic server and the hosted endpoint.\n\nScope, as the note's slice list states it:\n1. The view item in the Rules scope gains recipe sources: locators,\n   note facts named by hash and by room, and a package's projections.\n   Seven kinds: table, list, grid, timeline, compare, image, brief,\n   beside the five chart kinds of the chat-composed views note.\n2. The host mounts a recipe for a member over the rooms that member\n   reads; a source in a room the member does not read appears as\n   unreadable with the room named and nothing else shown. Two recipes\n   naming the same hashes are recipes for one thing, and the host says\n   so.\n3. A mark made in a view is a note act placed by locator, in the room\n   the member chooses, signed by the host's server as any act from a\n   view.\n4. A recipe kept in a shared room names a private note by its fact\n   reference and never by the digest of its text (approval 1703cf3b\n   point 5: a text digest would let the other party confirm a short\n   private mark by hashing guesses). The note's section 9 text is\n   corrected to say so in this head.\n5. Witness W2 (one list, two recipes), as the note's table states it:\n   both recipes mount for their keepers and are named as one thing;\n   A's private mark is unreadable to B with A's room named; row six in\n   the shared room shows B's mark with B as author.\n\nNot in this slice: consent (BO3), closings and the matter projection\n(BO4).","body":{"conditions":"Recipe sources by locator and fact reference, seven kinds; unreadable sources name the room only; private notes named by fact reference, never text digest, and the note corrected; witness W2 recorded; gate green.","target_head":"8659af4656b9289d25f239d02b22b89ae44fe658","target_ref"